    Section(
      title: '+ Explain the complex pain physiology in sickle cell disease and the evaluation of avascular necrosis as a contributor to chronic pain.',
      content:
          '**Pain Pathophysiology in SCD — A Complex, Multi-Mechanism Problem**:\n\n**1. Nociceptive Pain (tissue damage)**:\n- **Avascular necrosis (AVN)**: Caused by sickling-induced microvascular occlusion of end-arteriolar blood supply to bone. The femoral head is most commonly affected (50-70% of HbSS patients by age 35). Causes progressive bone death, articular surface collapse, and secondary osteoarthritis. This patient has bilateral hip AVN — a major source of mechanical, activity-related pain.\n- **Bone marrow infarction**: Repeated microinfarctions in the medullary bone of vertebrae, pelvis, and long bones cause chronic deep bone pain.\n- **Joint effusions**: Inflammatory response to microinfarction causes synovitis.\n\n**2. Neuropathic Pain**:\n- SCD patients develop **peripheral neuropathy** from chronic ischemic nerve damage. Prevalence up to 30%.\n- **Central sensitization**: Repeated severe pain episodes (crises since childhood) cause neuroplastic changes in the dorsal horn and brain, lowering pain thresholds and amplifying pain signals. This explains why chronic pain persists even between crises and why patients describe pain as worsening over years despite stable disease.\n- **Hyperalgesia**: Studies show SCD patients have lower pain thresholds and tolerance to experimental pain stimuli, confirming central sensitization.\n\n**3. Inflammatory Pain**:\n- Chronic hemolysis releases free hemoglobin, which scavenges nitric oxide, causing endothelial dysfunction, inflammation, and pain signaling.\n- Elevated substance P, calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP), and other inflammatory mediators.\n- Mast cell activation — increasingly recognized in SCD pain.\n\n**AVN Evaluation**:\n- **Staging (Ficat classification)**:\n  - Stage I: Normal X-ray, abnormal MRI (bone marrow edema)\n  - Stage II: Sclerosis/cysts on X-ray, femoral head shape preserved\n  - Stage III: Subchondral collapse (crescent sign) — femoral head flattening begins\n  - Stage IV: Acetabular involvement — secondary osteoarthritis\n- **This patient**: Order AP and frog-leg lateral hip X-rays. If Stage III-IV, total hip arthroplasty (THA) consultation is appropriate. THA in SCD patients is technically challenging (hard, sclerotic bone, increased infection risk, perioperative crisis risk) but can dramatically improve pain and function.\n- **MRI of bilateral hips**: If X-rays are equivocal or to assess extent of necrosis for surgical planning.\n- **Shoulder AVN**: Emerging — order shoulder X-rays and MRI if clinical suspicion is high.\n\n**Key Concept**: This patient\'s chronic pain is NOT simply \'drug-seeking behavior.\' It reflects a biologically complex condition with identifiable structural disease (AVN), peripheral nerve damage, central sensitization from years of repeated severe pain, and chronic inflammatory mediators. The pain is REAL, MEASURABLE, and UNDERTREATED.',
