    Section(
      title: '+ Outline a multimodal pain management plan for chronic SCD pain, including opioid optimization and non-opioid strategies.',
      content:
          '**Multimodal Pain Management Plan**:\n\n**1. Opioid Optimization (NOT Elimination)**:\n- She is on 90 MME daily with 30-40% relief. This is a moderate dose and she has clear structural disease justifying opioid use.\n- **Do NOT taper her opioids** without a clear alternative for pain control. Forced opioid reduction in SCD patients increases ED utilization, undertreated crises, and suffering.\n- **Convert to long-acting opioid**: Switch from oxycodone IR 15 mg QID to extended-release oxycodone (OxyContin) 30 mg BID for stable baseline coverage, plus oxycodone IR 10-15 mg Q4-6H PRN for breakthrough. This provides more consistent plasma levels and may improve pain control.\n- **Individualized crisis plan**: Written crisis action plan — at onset of crisis, increase opioid dose by 50-100%, add acetaminophen, hydrate aggressively, and present to ED if not improving in 6-8 hours. This empowers her to manage early crises at home.\n\n**2. Non-Opioid Pharmacologic Additions**:\n- **Duloxetine 60 mg daily**: SNRI for central sensitization component and comorbid depression. Dual benefit.\n- **Gabapentin 300 mg TID** (titrate to 600 mg TID): For neuropathic component (burning, tingling pain).\n- **Acetaminophen 1000 mg TID scheduled**: Adjuvant analgesic — reduces overall opioid need.\n- **NSAIDs**: Use cautiously — renal concerns in SCD. Short courses during crises if renal function is normal.\n- **Topical agents**: Diclofenac gel to bilateral hips for localized AVN pain. Lidocaine patches to lumbar spine.\n\n**3. AVN Management**:\n- **Orthopedic referral**: If AVN is Stage III-IV, total hip arthroplasty should be discussed. Bilateral hip replacement in a 22-year-old is a significant decision but may be the single most impactful intervention for her pain.\n- **If early-stage AVN**: Protected weight-bearing (cane use), physical therapy for hip strengthening and ROM preservation, bisphosphonates (limited evidence in SCD-related AVN).\n- **Activity modification**: Low-impact exercise — swimming, stationary cycling. Avoid impact loading.\n\n**4. Non-Pharmacologic Strategies**:\n- **Physical therapy**: Aquatic therapy for hip pain (buoyancy reduces joint loading). Core strengthening. Gentle hip ROM. Should NOT be prescriptive exercise that increases pain.\n- **CBT for pain**: Cognitive behavioral therapy specifically adapted for SCD pain — address catastrophizing, develop coping strategies, manage depression and anxiety. Evidence supports CBT as adjunct in SCD pain.\n- **Mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR)**: Moderate evidence for chronic pain management. Available as app-based programs for accessibility.\n- **Acupuncture**: Some evidence for chronic pain; may be worth trialing.\n- **Heat therapy**: Warm compresses to painful areas. Heated blankets during crises. Avoid cold (triggers sickling).\n\n**5. SCD Disease Modification**:\n- **Optimize hydroxyurea**: Check current dose and response. Target MCV >100 fL and HbF >20% if possible. Higher HbF reduces sickling and crisis frequency.\n- **Crizanlizumab (Adakveo)**: Anti-P-selectin antibody that reduces vaso-occlusive crises. Consider if she is having >3 crises/year.\n- **L-glutamine (Endari)**: FDA-approved for SCD. Reduces crisis frequency by approximately 25%.\n- **Chronic transfusion therapy**: Consider if crises remain frequent despite hydroxyurea optimization.\n\n**6. Mental Health**:\n- Depression treatment: Duloxetine (as above — addresses pain and depression). Psychology referral for CBT.\n- Address healthcare-related trauma: Her experiences of medical racism have caused healthcare avoidance and mistrust. This needs specific attention.\n- Academic support: Connect with disability services at her university for accommodations (flexible attendance, extended test time, rest breaks).',
